"BACKGROUND Nutrition interventions typically rely on self-reported intake that may be susceptible to differential      
reporting bias due to exposure to the intervention. Such differences may result from increased social desirability,     
increased attention to eating or improved recall accuracy, and may bias estimates of the intervention effect. This study
investigated differential reporting bias of fruit and vegetable intake in youth with type 1 diabetes participating in a 
randomized controlled trial targeting increased whole plant food intake. METHODS Participants (treatment                
n\u2009=\u200966, control n\u2009=\u200970) completed 3-day food records at baseline, 6-,12-, and 18-months, from which 
